{:edb/db-before-t 2485 :edb/db-after-t 2486 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535798 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:25:20.645Z" 13194139535798 true] [17592186129019 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366712 13194139535798 true] [17592186129019 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366733 13194139535798 true] [17592186129021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366719 13194139535798 true] [17592186129021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366726 13194139535798 true] [17592186129021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366740 13194139535798 true] [17592186366712 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366713 13194139535798 true] [17592186366712 1089 "q-104419" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366712 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535798 true] [17592186366712 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/assets/ae097e5f002d970ffe7393cacce708d431f4ef1f46c5a2c95e3fa1f5846d2c7d.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366712 1113 #uuid "e66f2c1c-a94b-5afe-ae18-b6390689133c" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366712 1145 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(2)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=4.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(4)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)\\ne f(4)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=4$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(1)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-1$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally, the function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=3$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ and $x=4$ only." 13194139535798 true] [17592186366713 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366714 13194139535798 true] [17592186366713 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366715 13194139535798 true] [17592186366713 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366716 13194139535798 true] [17592186366713 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366717 13194139535798 true] [17592186366713 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366718 13194139535798 true] [17592186366713 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535798 true] [17592186366713 1041 "selection" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366713 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366716 13194139535798 true] [17592186366713 1110 #uuid "4129dafb-6e62-56bc-b31b-dac8d699d22a" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366714 1034 "$x=2$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366714 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366714 1111 #uuid "f03d32e3-2489-5993-a57e-aa208bd12465" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366715 1034 "$x=2$ and $x=3$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366715 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366715 1111 #uuid "9282a665-b4cb-5be5-9d4d-e69c05c2f4fa" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366716 1034 "$x=2$ and $x=4$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366716 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366716 1111 #uuid "242e2566-f841-5914-8fd2-d09e23fe77b2" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366717 1034 "$x=-1$ and $x=3$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366717 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366717 1111 #uuid "c46dc117-1222-557b-a17c-5e2d1ec23648" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366718 1034 "$x=4$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366718 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366718 1111 #uuid "8b0351aa-de1a-5cf0-bc4c-c6833fb4eec1" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366719 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366720 13194139535798 true] [17592186366719 1089 "q-48120" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366719 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535798 true] [17592186366719 1100 "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-1 & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ 2(x-1), & x>1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}3x+2, & x<1 \\\\ 4, & x=1 \\\\ -3x+8, & x>1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-1}, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366719 1113 #uuid "f08cee0d-f749-5e56-a39d-99fa204b19b2" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366719 1145 "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=1:$\n\n- $f(1)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={1}^{3}-1=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =2(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=1:$\n\n- $g(1)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & =3(1)+2=5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & =-3(1)+8=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=5.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)\\ne g(1)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=1$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=1:$\n\n- $h(1)={e}^{(1-1)}=1$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}h(x) & ={e}^{(1-1)}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}h(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}h(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}h(x)=h(1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo, the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, only $g(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=1.$" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366721 13194139535798 true] [17592186366720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366722 13194139535798 true] [17592186366720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366723 13194139535798 true] [17592186366720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366724 13194139535798 true] [17592186366720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366725 13194139535798 true] [17592186366720 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535798 true] [17592186366720 1041 "selection" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366720 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366724 13194139535798 true] [17592186366720 1110 #uuid "41002051-f769-5b30-b6d1-d0945b88e5c7" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366721 1034 "$f(x)$ and $g(x)$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366721 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366721 1111 #uuid "a76f8129-1676-55b0-a8fc-af97551fb634" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366722 1034 "$f(x)$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366722 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366722 1111 #uuid "6599e070-e6ac-5600-8194-8e03a9ed440e" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366723 1034 "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366723 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366723 1111 #uuid "9454118f-c8d7-52a9-abfc-aaffae21de7c" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366724 1034 "$g(x)$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366724 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366724 1111 #uuid "4ad59a51-e359-5bc5-8bf2-0991e32220b2" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366725 1034 "$h(x)$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366725 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366725 1111 #uuid "4ca873b1-9369-5163-bfa7-9a8ee1f9110f" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366726 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366727 13194139535798 true] [17592186366726 1089 "q-48123" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366726 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535798 true] [17592186366726 1100 "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+3 & x<-1 \\\\ 4, & x=-1 \\\\ 3x+4, & x>-1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1), & x<-1 \\\\ 0, & x=-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-1, & x>-1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1, & x\\le -1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x+1, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366726 1113 #uuid "a7fcd771-d957-5c34-bd60-9ce3836ceaea" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366726 1145 "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x) & =2(-1)^{3}+3=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x) & =3(-1)+4=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $g(-1)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1+1)=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x) & =(-1)^{2}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=g(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo,  the function $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $h(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}h(x) & =(-1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}h(x) & =2(-1)^{2}+(-1)+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}h(x)=2.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}h(x)=h(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo,  the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, only $f(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=-1.$" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366727 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366728 13194139535798 true] [17592186366727 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366729 13194139535798 true] [17592186366727 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366730 13194139535798 true] [17592186366727 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366731 13194139535798 true] [17592186366727 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366732 13194139535798 true] [17592186366727 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535798 true] [17592186366727 1041 "selection" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366727 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366728 13194139535798 true] [17592186366727 1110 #uuid "a234af1b-c94e-5649-b7b8-320b90e2ae3d" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366728 1034 "$f(x)$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366728 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366728 1111 #uuid "0023f1b9-6c00-567a-a7cf-e5921a5a56af" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366729 1034 "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366729 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366729 1111 #uuid "e3e08aeb-7145-54b9-aad5-bbf0696d2ff9" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366730 1034 "$g(x)$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366730 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366730 1111 #uuid "c58e21ab-9d20-59c7-bcdf-0232f6160590" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366731 1034 "$h(x)$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366731 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366731 1111 #uuid "eed26057-9bcd-5424-989a-fa8eab8884ad" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366732 1034 "$f(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366732 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535798 true] [17592186366732 1111 #uuid "dcc4d0b4-4b54-5744-bc72-35f147f1460b" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366733 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366734 13194139535798 true] [17592186366733 1089 "q-71067" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366733 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535798 true] [17592186366733 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/assets/d036e94f6a07c526c9609ca2030cc785927562b0771335bbe12aa5fe27c3e369.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366733 1113 #uuid "0ad5208d-6613-538b-805c-388d7abb0069" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366733 1145 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-3)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)\\ne f(-3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nA similar argument applies for $x=3,$ which is also a removable discontinuity. \n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=3$ only." 13194139535798 true] [17592186366734 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366735 13194139535798 true] [17592186366734 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366736 13194139535798 true] [17592186366734 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366737 13194139535798 true] [17592186366734 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366738 13194139535798 true] [17592186366734 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366739 13194139535798 true] [17592186366734 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535798 true] [17592186366734 1041 "selection" 13194139535798 true] [17592186366734 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366735 13194139535798 true] [17592186366734 1110 #u